*** Opener's rebid after a one-level response over an overcall

A one-level new suit by responder after the overcall is natural and forcing,
exactly as without interference (the rows above), so opener rebids with the
uncontested table for the same two suits (NOTES.md #C5): the overcall only
takes away calls that were not in those tables anyway.

1C-(1D)-1H-(P)-
#PASTE rebid-1c-1h

1C-(1D)-1S-(P)-
#PASTE rebid-1c-1s

1C-(1H)-1S-(P)-
#PASTE rebid-1c-1s

1D-(1H)-1S-(P)-
#PASTE rebid-1d-1s

A two-level new suit by responder over the overcall is forcing (11+, a five-card
suit), and there is no uncontested table with the same meaning, so opener's rebid
is written once and pasted per auction: a raise (three-card support for a major,
four for a minor), notrump with a stopper in the overcall's suit (2NT 12--14,
3NT 18--21), and otherwise the cheapest rebid of the opening suit, which is
opener's "nothing else to say" call and so carries no requirement of its own
beyond the opening (NOTES.md #C5).

#CUT rebid-over-new-suit
3\N = {prio:4} \L+!\n
2N = {prio:3} 12--14 hcp, stopper
3N = {prio:3} 18--21 hcp, stopper
  #PASTE after-chain
\R = 12+ hcp
#ENDCUT

