** Balancing

A reopening bid means much the same as a direct-seat bid, but it can be lighter,
especially the takeout double and the 1NT overcall. The plain suit overcall
outranks the double (review finding: with no exclusion the double, appearing
first, otherwise won every tied-priority hand that also held a real 4-card
suit, so a hand worth a genuine reopening overcall was shown as a takeout
double instead); the jump overcall is given a distinct, lower-strength band
(6--10, not 1Y's own 6--16) and a six-card suit, like the direct seat's weak
jump, so it is a genuine preemptive jump with extra length (review findings:
sharing 1Y's full range meant *any* 5+ card hand jumped, and with a 5+ card
suit every 6--10 five-card hand still jumped). The balancing double, like the
direct one, promises shortness in their suit unless it is strong (17+).

(1X)-P-(P)-
1Y = {prio:1} 6--16 hcp, 4+#
D = {prio:-1} 8--16 hcp and 0--2X or 17+ hcp
1N = {prio:1} 10--15 hcp, bal, stopper
2Y = {prio:2} 6--10 hcp, 6+#

** Unmodeled interference reverts to natural bidding

The booklet's general rule (quoted at the top of this file) is that an
opponent's bid or double cancels the partnership's conventions and bids revert
to natural. Where the opponents interfere at a point this file has no table
for, the history lines below give that interference a position of its own with
no rows, marked by the `(any)` wildcard (any call other than the pass the
following tables already describe). A position with no rows is off-system, so
the next call is bid by natural inference -- rather than read "as if they had
passed" against the uncontested table, where most hands fit no row once the
interference has made some of its calls illegal and the rest inappropriate
(NOTES.md #C4).

